    {
      "name": "wordcraft",
      "repoUrl": "https://github.com/Blenux/wordcraft",
      "branches": [
        {
          "branch": "main",
          "commits": [
            {
              "message": "ODT: columns stating only their shares keep their proportions (gap 19) (#5722)\n\n* ODT gap 19: columns stating only their shares keep their proportions\n\nA table's columns giving only style:rel-column-width, as LibreOffice's CV\ntemplate does, were read as equal columns: wrong on screen, and written back\nthat way once anything rewrote the table. Divide the table's width by the\nshares when no column states a width.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* ODT: table column shares divide the right width (gap 19)\n\nAn aligned table's stated width is what shares divide, however wide. A column with\nonly a width takes it first, and the shares divide the rest, as in LibreOffice. A\ncolumn with no share takes the default style's. From CodeRabbit on this PR, checked\nin LibreOffice 26.8.2.2: a share on every column still goes before a stated width.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* ODT: a table in a cell shares out no page width (gap 19)\n\nA share-only table with nothing to divide, or one inside a cell, frame or narrower column with widths stated too, was given the page's width: an inner table got two 306 pt columns in a 100 pt cell. It now splits equally as main does. Also puts a doc comment back on its function.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Gap 19: read the column and row table defaults through one helper\n\nMain's row default (keep-together) beside this PR's column default took the style\nreader one over clippy's complexity limit; both now go through `read_table_default`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Gap 19: docs status lines are main's, with this change in front\n\nMerging main had kept this branch's older status lines in ROADMAP.md and\ndocs/file-format-parity.md, which set main's 2026-11-08 date back and dropped\nmain's newer entries and the parity doc's CLI path note.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Gap 19: an aligned table's shares go before stated widths; no column under 6 pt\n\nLibreOffice 26.8 lays out an aligned table whose columns give both a width and a share\nby the shares (measured). A share too small for a column gave a width the model\nrejects, losing the whole grid to the equal split; it now gives the narrowest column.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Gap 19: the progress log says which share-only tables keep their proportions\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nCo-authored-by: FredApps <FredApps@users.noreply.github.com>",
              "url": "https://github.com/Blenux/wordcraft/commit/05f781cfdac1d89034c0f4f5824b597ff845f2ef",
              "date": "2026-11-09T15:35:36Z"
            },
            {
              "message": "ODT: a font size reaches every script's text (gap 19) (#5728)\n\n* ODT gap 19: a font size reaches every script's text\n\nOnly fo:font-size was written, so Asian and complex-script text kept the default\nsize in LibreOffice: Chinese text set to 24 pt stayed at 12. Write the Asian and\ncomplex sizes with it, as bold and italic are, and read a complex size equal to\nthe text's as no size of its own.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* ODT: a complex size like the text's stays over a style or default with another (gap 19)\n\nDropped as no size of its own, the default's showed instead: a 24 pt Arabic run drew\nat 12, and a heading saved its complex size as 12. It's now dropped only where that\nchanges nothing, and a default stating one size for all goes with that size.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* ODT: a complex size only goes when no run's complex size changes (gap 19)\n\nThe check looked at each style alone, so a parent's complex size went from under a child or run that states only a size, and the size stated alone then showed through. The default's and the styles' now go together, and stay as the file says if any run's complex size would change.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Gap 19: docs status lines are main's, with this change in front\n\nMerging main had kept this branch's older status lines in ROADMAP.md and\ndocs/file-format-parity.md, which set main's 2026-11-08 date back and dropped\nmain's newer entries and the parity doc's CLI path note.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Gap 19: a size stated alone keeps the complex size above it when saved\n\nA run or paragraph style stating only a size took that size as its complex-script size\non save, though the model takes it from what is above it. The writer now writes the\ncomplex size the model resolves where it differs from the size.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nCo-authored-by: FredApps <FredApps@users.noreply.github.com>",
              "url": "https://github.com/Blenux/wordcraft/commit/8a97427f51f1b17cf7d006047180ae958faa09e9",
              "date": "2026-11-09T15:34:47Z"
            },
            {
              "message": "ODT: text can turn off what its style turns on (gap 19) (#5724)\n\n* ODT gap 19: text can turn off what its style turns on\n\nA word not bold in a bold heading showed bold on screen, and came back bold in\nLibreOffice after a save: an \"off\" on a run was dropped because the run's own\nformatting said nothing, so the paragraph's or character style's showed through.\nKeep it on the run for bold, italic, underline, strike-through, capitals and\nsmall capitals, and write it back.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* ODT: an Asian or complex normal says nothing of Latin text (gap 19)\n\nWith \"off\" now kept on the text, `style:font-weight-complex=\"normal\"` alone took a\nword's bold in a bold heading, and saved it as `fo:font-weight=\"normal\"`. Those\nattributes stay fallbacks only to turn it on.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* ODT: strike-through turned off over a double one stays off (gap 19)\n\nA style or span saying line-through none over a double strike still showed the double strike, on screen and after a save.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Docs: a missing full stop in the ODT revision row (gap 19)\n\nDocs only.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* ODT: a span's character style wins over its paragraph turning off (gap 19)\n\nA paragraph saying not bold made a span in a bold character style not bold too, on screen and after a save. The span's style now wins, for bold, italic, underline, strike-through and capitals.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Gap 19: docs status lines are main's, with this change in front\n\nMerging main had kept this branch's older status lines in ROADMAP.md and\ndocs/file-format-parity.md, which set main's 2026-11-08 date back and dropped\nmain's newer entries and the parity doc's CLI path note.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nCo-authored-by: FredApps <FredApps@users.noreply.github.com>",
              "url": "https://github.com/Blenux/wordcraft/commit/92eac2aa7c8e54232f25ea68fd61d43f3cd0079e",
              "date": "2026-11-09T15:33:58Z"
            },
            {
              "message": "ODT: custom styles with an outline level keep their name when edited (gap 19) (#5770)\n\n* ODT gap 19: custom styles with an outline level keep their name when edited\n\nA paragraph in a custom style with style:default-outline-level was read as\nWordCraft's Heading 1 or whichever level it named. Unedited, it was written as\nread. Edited, it was saved as Heading_20_1, so LibreOffice showed WordCraft's\nblue heading instead of the file's look, and WordCraft drew it that way too.\n\nRead such a style as a custom style that carries the outline level. The\nparagraph stays a heading at that level in WordCraft, is numbered with the\nfile's outline as LibreOffice numbers it, and is saved as\n<text:h text:style-name=\"Head\" text:outline-level=\"1\">. The level comes from\nthe style, not the paragraph kind, because kinds stop at Heading 6.\n\nThe writer dropped a custom style's name on any heading, title, quotation or\ncode paragraph, so custom styles based on those lost their look when edited\ntoo. It now keeps the name for every kind.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* ODT: a heading's own outline level gives its number (gap 19)\n\nLibreOffice numbers a heading by its text:outline-level. WordCraft took the\nnumber from the style's level, so a heading stating another showed the wrong\nnumber, or one on a level the outline doesn't number. A heading in a list stays\nthe list's. Found by CodeRabbit on this PR, as was the Title-based style test.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* ODT: a heading in a list of its own keeps it, and an unnumbered level takes no number (gap 19)\n\nAny list whose first level was linked to a style counted as the outline's, so a\nheading deeper in one lost its list on save. Now only the number the outline\nitself gives counts. A style based on a numbered heading, at a level the outline\ndoesn't number, took its parent's number. Found by CodeRabbit on this PR.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* ODT: a heading past level 6 keeps its list (gap 19)\n\nThe written outline has no number past Heading 6, so counting such a heading's\nlist as the outline's lost its number. Found by CodeRabbit on this PR.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* ODT: a style with a heading numbered by its own level stays on the outline (gap 19)\n\nWriting it with no list style took the number from that heading in LibreOffice.\nWritten so, the numbers match the file's. Found by CodeRabbit on this PR.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* ODT: a heading past level six numbered through its style keeps its list (gap 19)\n\nThe written outline numbers only six levels, and the list was dropped for a heading numbered only through its style, so at levels 7 to 9 it lost its number once its style was rewritten.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Gap 19: docs status lines are main's, with this change in front\n\nMerging main had kept this branch's older status lines in ROADMAP.md and\ndocs/file-format-parity.md, which set main's 2026-11-08 date back and dropped\nmain's newer entries and the parity doc's CLI path note.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nCo-authored-by: FredApps <FredApps@users.noreply.github.com>",
              "url": "https://github.com/Blenux/wordcraft/commit/29a5abf3e7a43002bf132bb52e21fc230b6374bc",
              "date": "2026-11-09T15:16:06Z"
            },
            {
              "message": "ODT: pictures keep the size their file gives (gap 19) (#5721)\n\n* ODT gap 19: pictures keep the size their file gives\n\nOpening a file fitted a picture wider than the text to the text, and shrank any\npicture wider than 6.5 in to that, even with its size stated. So a save with no\nedit to it took its paragraph off being written as read, and LibreOffice laid\nit out differently. Pictures in OpenDocument files keep the size they state.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* ODT: a picture keeps a size stated on one side, in proportion (gap 19)\n\nOnly a frame stating both sides kept them. On one side alone a picture past a\ncolumn's width was still fitted to it, and past 1,584 pt each side was cut\nseparately, which distorted it. A size past any page can no longer overflow the\nsizing either. Found by CodeRabbit on this PR.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* ODT: an inline picture with no stated size is fitted to the text (gap 19)\n\nSkipping the fit for OpenDocument files left one wider than the text on a small\npage, which `main` fits. A size the file states still stands, and a test now fails\nwithout that. The fit happens when it is read, so its paragraph stays as read.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* ODT: an object's picture is fitted too, and one side follows the picture's own shape (gap 19)\n\nAn inline object with no stated size kept its picture's width on a small page, which main fitted. A picture stated on one side took the other from the size already capped to the page: 10 by 1000 pixels at 500 pt wide came out 468 by 1584 pt, not 15.84 by 1584.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* ODT: a frame stating a size of nothing is fitted like one stating none (gap 19)\n\nsvg:width=\"0pt\" counted as a stated size for fitting, though the picture then came at its own size, so it could run past the text on a small page.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* ODT: a picture in line that is raised, turned or mirrored is fitted too (gap 19)\n\nSuch a picture has a float record that is still in line, which the reader's fit skipped, so with no size stated it stayed 468 pt wide on a small page. main fitted it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Gap 19: docs status lines are main's, with this change in front\n\nMerging main had kept this branch's older status lines in ROADMAP.md and\ndocs/file-format-parity.md, which set main's 2026-11-08 date back and dropped\nmain's newer entries and the parity doc's CLI path note.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Order the parity header's change notes newest first\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nCo-authored-by: FredApps <FredApps@users.noreply.github.com>",
              "url": "https://github.com/Blenux/wordcraft/commit/bd9e726cdaae18f73a29b57324e4833b033fd98f",
              "date": "2026-11-09T15:15:23Z"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
